+++
locale = "en"
term = "itera-filum-return-thread"
kind = "reject"
category = "loop"
grammar = ["itera_stmt", "apud_clause", "filum_clause", "redde_stmt", "expression"]
canonical = false
summary = "`return thread` ends one lane of a `thread` loop; written anywhere else it is refused."
no_expected = "rejects"
diagnostic = "SEM010:return_thread_outside_lane"
+++

# This exemplum intentionally fails to compile: `return thread` (la `redde
# filum`) ends the current lane of a `thread` loop, from any depth of that lane's
# body. Outside a lane body there is no lane to end, so it is refused. It is
# refused too when it carries a value (`return_thread_with_value`: lanes deliver
# results through their stores) and inside a closure or reduction body nested in
# the lane (`return_thread_in_nested_body`: that body is not the lane). Plain
# `return` in a lane body stays refused (`return_in_thread_body`).
fn run() → void {
    return thread
}

main {
    run()
}
